From 09336e5014f0a6b72b7a00453f019c1e9f50e15e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BANCO audit <waelzaid66@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 20:12:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] proof: P0-5 steps 3-6 - the detached case reaches the
 contract and the clients

For verification only, on the audit proof branch. Completes the item whose
migration and media reclamation landed in 4cefc2f.

Four response types now say what the database does: BookingDTO,
ConversationSummaryDTO, AdminLeadRow and ReportRow carry listing_id as
nullable, because migration 0008 lets those rows survive their listing
detached. Two joins that keyed on the id now drop the nulls rather than
querying for them - a booking with no listing has no host to name, and a
detached conversation has no thumbnail.

The spec follows: five schemas gain nullable: true (Lead,
ConversationSummary, Report, Booking, BookingListItem) and codegen carries
that into the typed client and the zod schemas. Story and ImportOrder were
already declared nullable and were left alone; Ad and Comment stay
non-null because those tables still cascade.

Client audit, measured rather than estimated: of the 98 references to
listing_id across six surfaces, zero fail to typecheck and four needed a
change - the booking card link on mobile and on both web surfaces built
the href /listing/null, and the conversation row passed listingId: null
into a route whose guards only test for presence, where it would serialise
as the string "null". LeadsPanel was already guarded on both surfaces.
admin-os now shows its own em-dash convention for a deleted listing.

Two chain pins added, both mutation-tested: the schema shape (comments
stripped, so prose cannot satisfy it) and the migration's registration at
idx 8. Restoring the cascade on one table fails the first; unregistering
the migration fails the second. Gate 245 to 247.

pnpm run typecheck now exits 0 from the root. API suite 518 passed / 3
skipped / 0 failed via the official runner on a database created for the
run, Gate-4 6/6, chain 247/247, confidence 24/24, parity 40/40, both web
packages 32/32.
